  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The current approach is gradient-based optimization, which is likely stuck in local optima. We switch to a genetic algorithm (GA) with elitism and local refinement. The GA explores diverse solutions through crossover and mutation, followed by local optimization to refine promising candidates. This hybrid approach balances global exploration and local exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Objective: Minimize overlap integral C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i, \u2211h[i] = n/2\n   - Representation: h as a real-valued vector with 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n   - Fitness: -C\u2085 for minimization\n   - Key operations:\n        * Crossover: Blend crossover for real numbers\n        * Mutation: Uniform perturbation\n        * Repair: Adjust mass fraction by modifying random elements\n        * Local optimization: Guided hill climb using worst-shift gradient\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n        i. Create initial population of size 100:\n           - If parent provided: Include parent and 99 random vectors\n           - Else: Generate all 100 vectors randomly\n        ii. Repair all vectors to satisfy \u2211h[i]=n/2\n        iii. Set best_fitness = minimum C\u2085 across population\n\n   b. Optimization loop:\n        i. For each generation:\n           1. Tournament selection (tournament size=3)\n           2. Crossover (blend type with random alpha) with 70% probability\n           3. Mutation: Random perturbation of 10% of genes\n           4. Repair all offspring\n           5. Potentially trigger local optimization if new best found:\n               - If improvement threshold (C\u2085 decrease >= 1e-4) achieved\n               - Only if >5% budget remains\n           6. Elitism: Keep best individual unchanged\n        ii. Monitor elapsed time for termination\n\n   c. Termination conditions:\n        i. Max 500 generations\n        ii. Time budget exhausted\n        iii. No improvement over 10 generations with insufficient time left\n\n   d. Validation:\n        i. Verify feasible solution at end (all h[i] in [0,1], \u2211h[i]=n/2)\n        ii. Return best h, C\u2085, n=192\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   a. Primary configuration:\n        - Population: 100 individuals\n        - Tournament size: 3\n        - Crossover probability: 0.7\n        - Mutation probability: 0.1\n        - Generations: 500 maximum\n\n   b. Key adaptation rule:\n        - Increase mutation rate to 0.3 if no improvement in 20 generations\n\n   c. Local optimization ties non-parent time proportionally to fitness\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   a. Each generation: 100*h_objective_evaluations\n   b. Typical time per h_objective: ~0.1s at n=192\n   c. Schedule:\n        - Late termination if pre-set time exceeded\n        - Early exit if no improvement despite sufficient time\n        - Repair occurs automatically via @enforce_sum()\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   a. Final checks:\n        i. 0.0 \u2264 min(h[i]) \u2264 max(h[i]) \u2264 1.0\n        ii. abs(\u2211h[i] - n/2) < 1e-8\n        iii. Return C\u2085 from np.correlate() calculation\n\n   b. Output format:\n        [h.tolist(), float(final_C5), int(n)]\n\n   c. Implementation tips:\n        - Use np.clip to restrict values\n        - Avoid recomputing repair if possible\n        - Use original fitness evaluation (instead of logarithmic surrogate)",
